<header><p class="st-kicker">other · Mainland Southeast Asia</p><h1 class="st-title">Karenni States</h1><p class="st-meta">1820 CE – 1948 CE (approx.) · capital Loikaw</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A group of small Karenni principalities in the eastern hills of Burma whose independence Britain and Burma recognised in 1875.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The Karenni, or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karenni-people">Red Karen</a>, lived in the hills between the Shan plateau and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/lan-na">Lan Na</a>. In the early 19th century their chiefs organised several small states, the most important being <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kantarawaddy">Kantarawaddy</a>, alongside <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bawlake">Bawlake</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyebogyi">Kyebogyi</a>. Their early history is known mainly from British and Burmese records.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Karenni chiefs, called saopha in the Shan style, ruled over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a> farmers, hill cultivators and teak forests, and traded and raided across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/salween">Salween</a>. In 1875 an agreement between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mindon-min">King Mindon</a> of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/konbaung-dynasty">Konbaung dynasty</a> and the British recognised the Karenni states as independent of both, a status that made them unlike the Shan states.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>From 1892 the Karenni chiefs accepted British protection, but the states remained outside <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/british-burma">British Burma</a> proper. In 1948 they joined the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/myanmar">Union of Burma</a>, and in 1952 the region became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kayah-state">Kayah State</a>. Claims of separate status fuelled a long Karenni insurgency.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/karenni-states.jpg" alt="Karenni States" width="320" height="271"><figcaption>Image: Xufanc · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Map_of_Karenni_States-1917.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1820 CE – 1948 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>other</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Loikaw</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Animism, Christianity, Buddhism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Karenni (Kayah)</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/myanmar">Myanmar</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Kayah States, Karenni</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1884&amp;focus=karenni-states" aria-label="Karenni States on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7335 1492 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Karenni States lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M7655 1748L4997 651M7655 1748L7595 1702"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M4997 651h0M7595 1702h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7655 1748h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7655 1748h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=1884&amp;focus=karenni-states">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
